1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Right, well, for our presentation

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,610
shall I start with the early life of Thor Heyerdahl?

3
00:00:06,610 --> 00:00:10,780
Sure. Why don't you begin with describing the type of boy he was

4
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:13,170
especially his passion for collecting things.

5
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:16,009
That's right, he had his own little museum.

6
00:00:16,009 --> 00:00:20,480
And I think it's unusual for children to develop their own values

7
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,750
and not join in their parents' hobbies.

8
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:27,640
I'm thinking of how Heyerdahl wouldn't go hunting with his dad, for example.

9
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:31,870
Yeah, he preferred to learn about nature by listening to his mother read to him.

10
00:00:31,870 --> 00:00:37,300
And quite early on he knew he wanted to become an explorer when he grew up.

11
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:42,040
That came from his camping trips he went on in Norway I think...

12
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:46,000
No, it was climbing that he spent his time on as a young man.

13
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,466
Oh, right... After university he married a classmate and together,

14
00:00:50,466 --> 00:00:54,484
they decided to experience living on a small island,

15
00:00:54,484 --> 00:00:58,950
to find out how harsh weather conditions shaped people's lifestyles.

16
00:00:58,950 --> 00:01:02,240
As part of their preparation before they left home

17
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:06,340
they learnt basic survival skills like building a shelter.

18
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:12,344
I guess they needed that knowledge in order to live wild in a remote location with few inhabitants,

19
00:01:12,344 --> 00:01:16,680
cut off by the sea, which is what they were aiming to do.

20
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:26,190
An important part of your talk should be the radical theory Heyerdahl formed from examining mysterious ancient carvings that he happened to find on the island.

21
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:30,810
I think you should finish with that. -- OK.

22
00:01:30,810 --> 00:01:36,891
All right, Victor, so after your part I'll talk about Thor Heyerdahl's adult life,

23
00:01:36,891 --> 00:01:40,800
continuing from the theory he had about Polynesian migration.

24
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,103
Up until that time of course,

25
00:01:43,103 --> 00:01:49,630
academics had believed that humans first migrated to the islands in Polynesia from Asia, in the west.

26
00:01:49,630 --> 00:01:53,334
Yes, they thought that travel from the east was impossible,

27
00:01:53,334 --> 00:01:58,060
because of the huge, empty stretch of ocean that lies between the islands

28
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:00,000
and the nearest inhabited land.

29
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,467
Yes, but Heyerdahl spent ages studying the cloud movements,

30
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:08,090
ocean currents and wind patterns to find if it was actually possible.

31
00:02:08,090 --> 00:02:15,220
And another argument was that there was no tradition of large ship-building in the communities lying to the east of Polynesia.

32
00:02:15,220 --> 00:02:20,550
But Heyerdahl knew they made lots of coastal voyages in locally built canoes.

33
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:26,540
Yes, or sailing on rafts, as was shown by the long voyage that Heyerdahl did next.

34
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:29,268
It was an incredibly risky journey to undertake -

35
00:02:29,268 --> 00:02:33,700
sometimes I wonder if he did that trip for private reasons, you know?

36
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:39,090
To show others that he could have spectacular adventures. What do you think, Olivia?

37
00:02:39,090 --> 00:02:43,625
Well, I think it was more a matter of simply trying out his idea,

38
00:02:43,625 --> 00:02:46,540
to see if migration from the east was possible.

39
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:50,918
Yes, that's probably it. And the poor guy suffered a bit at that time

40
00:02:50,918 --> 00:02:54,670
because the war forced him to stop his work for some years...

41
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:58,280
Yes. When he got started again and planned his epic voyage,

42
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:03,530
do you think it was important to him that he achieve it before anyone else did?

43
00:03:03,530 --> 00:03:07,365
Um, I haven't read anywhere that that was his motivation.

44
00:03:07,365 --> 00:03:12,735
The most important factor seems to have been that he use only ancient techniques

45
00:03:12,735 --> 00:03:15,420
and local materials to build his raft.

46
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:18,550
Yes. I wonder how fast it went.

47
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:24,500
Well, it took them 97 days from South America to the Pacific Islands.

48
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:28,378
Mm. And after that, Heyerdahl went to Easter Island, didn't he?

49
00:03:28,378 --> 00:03:31,600
We should mention the purpose of that trip

50
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,430
I think he sailed there in a boat made out of reeds.

51
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:39,630
No, that was later on in Egypt, Olivia. -- Oh, yes, that's right.

52
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:44,833
But what he wanted to do was talk to the local people about their old stone carvings

53
00:03:44,833 --> 00:03:48,200
and then make one himself to learn more about the process.

54
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:50,800
I see. Well, what a great life.

55
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:54,520
Even though many of his theories have been disproven

56
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:58,230
he certainly left a lasting impression on many disciplines, didn't he?

57
00:03:58,230 --> 00:04:05,260
To my mind, he was the first person to establish what modern academics call practical archaeology.

58
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:09,900
I mean, that they try to recreate something from the past today

59
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:11,860
like he did with his raft trip.

60
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:15,260
It's unfortunate that his ideas about

61
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:19,000
where Polynesians originated from have been completely discredited.

62
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,730
Yes. Right, well, I'll prepare a PowerPoint slide at the end that acknowledges our sources.

63
00:04:25,730 --> 00:04:30,360
I mainly used The Life and Work of Thor Heyerdahl by William Oliver.

64
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:34,250
I thought the research methods he used were very sound,

65
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:38,530
although I must say I found the overall tone somewhat old-fashioned.

66
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:41,540
I think they need to do a new, revised edition.

67
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:47,140
Yeah, I agree. What about the subject matter - I found it really challenging!

68
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:49,610
Well, it's a complex issue...

69
00:04:49,610 --> 00:04:54,160
I thought the book had lots of good points. What did you think of...
